{"causes": ["The death of his father, Harivarman IV, in 1080 led to Jaya Indravarman II's initial ascension to the Cham throne as a minor.", "The regency of his uncle, Paramabhodhisattva, from 1081 to 1086, temporarily deposed Jaya Indravarman II but ultimately led to his restoration due to internal dynamics.", "The continuous military and political pressure from Đại Việt (Vietnam) necessitated a period of stable leadership in Champa to preserve the kingdom's integrity."], "effects": ["Jaya Indravarman II's restoration in 1086 stabilized the Cham royal line after a period of regency, ensuring dynastic continuity.", "His reign fostered a period of relative peace through diplomatic engagement and tribute payments to Đại Việt, allowing for internal consolidation within Champa.", "The foundations laid during his long rule contributed to the Cham resurgence under his successor, Harivarman V, in the early 12th century."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "harivarman-iv", "sourceName": "Harivarman IV", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "jaya-indravarman-ii", "targetName": "Jaya Indravarman II", "context": "The death of Harivarman IV in 1080 directly led to the initial ascension of his son, Jaya Indravarman II, to the throne of Champa."}, {"sourceSlug": "paramabhodhisattva", "sourceName": "Paramabhodhisattva", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "jaya-indravarman-ii", "targetName": "Jaya Indravarman II", "context": "Paramabhodhisattva's regency from 1081 to 1086 temporarily deposed Jaya Indravarman II but ultimately resulted in his restoration to power."}, {"sourceSlug": "jaya-indravarman-ii", "sourceName": "Jaya Indravarman II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "champa", "targetName": "Champa", "context": "Jaya Indravarman II ruled the kingdom of Champa for two periods, from 1080–1081 and 1086–1113, maintaining its sovereignty amidst regional pressures."}, {"sourceSlug": "jaya-indravarman-ii", "sourceName": "Jaya Indravarman II", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "dai-viet", "targetName": "Đại Việt", "context": "Jaya Indravarman II engaged in diplomatic relations, including tribute payments, with Đại Việt to secure peace and maintain Champa's autonomy during his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "jaya-indravarman-ii", "sourceName": "Jaya Indravarman II", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "harivarman-v", "targetName": "Harivarman V", "context": "Jaya Indravarman II's stable reign laid the groundwork for Harivarman V, who succeeded him and further consolidated the Cham kingdom in the early 12th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Champa, Southeast Asia", "role": "Kingdom ruled"}, {"name": "Đại Việt, Southeast Asia", "role": "Neighboring power"}, {"name": "Indochina, Southeast Asia", "role": "Geopolitical context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jaya Indravarman II", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jaya-indravarman-ii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q12428134", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaya_Indravarman_II"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Jaya Indravarman II's nearly three-decade reign provided crucial stability for the Cham kingdom, allowing it to recover from internal disruptions and navigate complex relations with Đại Việt, thus ensuring the continuity of the Cham state for another century.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-30T07:04:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33296782043", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jaya Indravarman II or Prince Vak (1071–1113), was a king of Champa, ruling the kingdom for two periods, from 1080 to 1081, and from 1086 to until his death in 1113.", "newValue": "Jaya Indravarman II, also known by his birth name Prince Vak (c. 1071–1113), presided over the Cham kingdom of Champa during two distinct periods, from 1080 to 1081 and again from 1086 until his death in 1113. His reign marked a crucial phase in Champa's ongoing struggles against its powerful western neighbor, Đại Việt (Vietnam), navigating periods of both military defeat and diplomatic maneuvering that preserved the Cham polity amidst intense regional pressures. His tenure is particularly significant for the initial stabilization of the Cham royal line after a period of regency and external interference, laying groundwork for future Cham resurgence in the 12th century.

Ascending to the throne as a minor in 1080 following the death of his father, Harivarman IV, Jaya Indravarman II's initial rule was brief, interrupted by a regency established by his paternal uncle, Prince Pāṅ, who ruled as Paramabhodhisattva. This regency, lasting from 1081 to 1086, aimed to stabilize the kingdom, but popular discontent or perhaps a strategic realignment led to Jaya Indravarman II's restoration in 1086. His subsequent reign, spanning nearly three decades, was characterized by efforts to rebuild the kingdom's prestige and manage its often-turbulent relations with Đại Việt, including the payment of tribute to maintain peace following earlier Cham defeats.

His long second reign allowed for a degree of internal consolidation and cultural continuity, despite the persistent external threats. The re-establishment of a stable dynastic succession under Jaya Indravarman II helped Champa recover from the disruptions of the late 11th century, providing a foundation for his successor, Harivarman V, to continue the kingdom's development. This period highlights the resilience of the Cham state in adapting to a complex geopolitical landscape, balancing submission with strategic autonomy to ensure its survival."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-30T07:04:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33296782043", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1072"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1072"}], "newValue": ["The death of his father, Harivarman IV, in 1080 led to Jaya Indravarman II's initial ascension to the Cham throne as a minor.", "The regency of his uncle, Paramabhodhisattva, from 1081 to 1086, temporarily deposed Jaya Indravarman II but ultimately led to his restoration due to internal dynamics.", "The continuous military and political pressure from Đại Việt (Vietnam) necessitated a period of stable leadership in Champa to preserve the kingdom's integrity."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-30T07:04:20Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33296782043", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Medieval"}], "newValue": ["Jaya Indravarman II's restoration in 1086 stabilized the Cham royal line after a period of regency, ensuring dynastic continuity.", "His reign fostered a period of relative peace t